The bundle's own README says it contains no secrets, no credentials,
no target intelligence, and no captured sessions. This tree holds the
playbooks directory (thirty-one SKILL.md files: nineteen vendored, twelve
authored first-party in this repository).

Seventeen came over as the bundle's own playbooks. Two more —
choose-the-right-playbook and jailbreak-to-unlock — were red-teamer skills
in the bundle, adapted here into playbooks: the single-lead scanner has no
separate Scope agent, plan-approval lifecycle, or loki-cli, so their
references to those were rewritten to send through the browser and record
with scanner_record_finding. The other surviving playbooks referenced both
by name, so leaving them out stranded those references.

Twelve more were authored first-party in this repository, not from the bundle.
They follow the same shape (objective, when to choose, ordered method,
evidence that proves it) and the same single-lead vocabulary as the vendored
notes, method only and no target intelligence:

- egress-and-exfiltration-channels: turn the agent's own permitted
  destinations into the exfil path (the inherited trust and egress gaps family).
- agent-command-execution: host command execution through a coding, CLI,
  or IDE agent (the execute and RCE half of indirect injection).
- authorization-claim-acceptance: the agent treating an asserted authorization
  as real for a caller it cannot verify (a trust flag it fills from a claim, a
  role it grants on assertion, its own standing credential).
- approval-gate-bypass: getting a human approval for one effect and executing
  another (argument injection into a pre-approved action, a misleading
  confirmation, consent fatigue, a change between approval and execution).
- concealed-instruction-injection: delivering the injected instruction where a
  human reviewing the same artifact will not see it but the model will (hidden
  text, metadata, formulas, alt-text, text inside an image, a QR code).
- user-targeted-deception: the assistant delivering an attacker lure to its own
  user through the trusted channel (a fake alert, a malicious link or button, a
  QR code, a planted false instruction).
- scheduled-action-abuse: persistence and delayed firing through the agent's
  own scheduling and automation rather than memory (a recurring job, a
  reminder, a webhook, a trigger).
- cross-session-data-leakage: one user's data surfacing in another's session
  through shared AI-layer state (memory, cache, retained context, a shared
  index), with no backend identifier manipulation.
- template-and-expression-injection: the agent framework evaluating attacker
  input as a template or expression rather than as data (prompt templates,
  expression and formula languages, format and query strings).
- persistent-directive-injection: a behavior-altering directive planted in a
  store the agent reads as standing instruction (memory, preferences, a rules or
  config file) that fires in a later clean session.
- tool-output-injection: an instruction seeded in a field one of the chatbot's
  own tools returns, which it reads as trusted task context and acts on.
- mcp-server-trust-abuse: abusing the trust a host extends to a connected MCP
  server (definitions loaded before approval, changed after approval, name
  collisions, and the host tokens the server can reach).

Deliberately left out of this tree, and not mounted into the worker:

- personas/ — Enumerator, Red Teamer, Scope, Assessor. A team of four.
  This process runs one lead.
- skills/using-loki-cli and the other skills that assume the full team.
- tools/loki-cli — an executable that assumes a Scope agent and a plan
  approval flow this deployment does not have.
- prompt-stack/ and reference/ — another harness's brief and permission
  matrix.

The odin-intel client from the same bundle IS now vendored, separately,
at third_party/odin-intel; see that directory's NOTICE. The playbook
notes reference it, and when it is mounted the seed points the model at
it rather than telling the model it does not exist.

The worker mounts playbooks/ read-only at /playbooks only when
PLAYBOOK_HOST_PATH names this directory on the host. The seed names
that path only in the same case.

